{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q026_protein_kinase_like_function_transfer","answer":"In SCOPe 2.08, all three catalytic domains are alpha+beta proteins in the protein-kinase-like fold d.144 and, more strongly, the single protein-kinase-like superfamily d.144.1. They remain distinct families: canonical protein-kinase catalytic subunits d.144.1.7, aminoglycoside phosphotransferases (APHs) d.144.1.6, and phosphoinositide 3-kinase catalytic domains d.144.1.4. Superfamily membership supports remote common ancestry—not merely a similar fold—and a conserved mechanistic core involving nucleotide binding and phosphoryl transfer. It does not establish identical detailed mechanisms or specificity: canonical protein kinases generally phosphorylate protein residues, APHs phosphorylate aminoglycosides, and PI3Ks phosphorylate phosphoinositide headgroups.

Substrate specificity must not be transferred across these families from fold/superfamily membership, catalytic-motif resemblance, or a structural hit alone. Such transfer needs target-specific evidence: robust sequence/profile and phylogenetic placement in the relevant family and preferably subfamily; conservation of substrate-contacting and catalytic residues; a target or close-family structure with appropriate domain boundaries and, ideally, bound nucleotide, metal, and substrate/product analogue; and biochemical assays establishing the acceptor, phosphorylation position, kinetics, cofactor dependence, and loss of activity after catalytic-site mutation. Cellular or genetic evidence is additionally needed to establish the physiological substrate.

Regulation is still less transferable because it often resides outside the catalytic core. Claims require evidence about the full-length domain architecture, regulatory domains and insertions, activation-loop or other conformational states, post-translational modifications, interaction partners, oligomerization, membrane recruitment/localization, and perturbation in the relevant cellular context. A distant d.144.1 template can support the gross catalytic fold and approximate nucleotide-binding site, but not side-chain-level substrate recognition, regulatory loops, or an active/inactive state. Template or representative selection should favor a same-family, preferably same-subfamily ortholog with high target-domain identity and coverage, correct ligand/conformational state, intact active-site coordinates, and suitable experimental quality; a SCOPe/ASTRAL representative is a structural or nonredundancy representative, not automatically a functional representative.","evidence_used":["mcp__scope__get_scop_service_info — /api/v1/; /api/v1/releases/current","mcp__scope__search_scop — /api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=aminoglycoside+phosphotransferase&mode=auto&limit=50","mcp__scope__get_scop_node — /api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/d.144; /api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/d.144/parents; /api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/d.144.1; /api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/d.144.1/parents","mcp__scope__list_scop_children — /api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/d.144/children?limit=100; /api/v1/releases/2.08/sccs/d.144.1/children?limit=100"]}]}
